#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253304 :question/problem "![](images/16/16d6786a4b1934ed234d4bf0896187aa6e93ec70cf045ddf4b743b1f51eaac2f.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-4,-3)$ and the point $X(-1,0)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{(-1-(-4))^{2}+(0-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253305 :question/problem "![](images/2b/2bd7e3cb9594623c6f5dc3fad35c33c71df0efe9e80b2053dde33f4ef9080bda.png)\n\nThe circle above is centered at $C(-4,-3)$ and the point $X(0,-1)$ lies on the circumference. Find the equation of the circle." :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe length of the radius is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}CX & =\\sqrt{(0-(-4))^{2}+(-1-(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our circle is generated by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-4))^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ (x+4)^{2}+(y+3)^{2} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253306 :question/problem "![](images/af/afd2f435a60fbb5a0f0f83c5f8965a74ca107d1ed4b14d03401f7966a323d2f1.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2,4)$ and $(2,0)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(2,4)$ and $Y(2,0),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(4-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{4}{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, we can find the center using the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{2+2}{2},\\frac{4+0}{2})=(2,2)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2$ and center $(a,b)=(2,2),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253307 :question/problem "![](images/08/08653e0cdc9bb404bd7ef193dfc9f1b5c0884002e83dd72cbcbda5779a2363a1.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(2,-1)$ and $Y(2,1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(2-2)^{2}+(-1-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{XY}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, we can find the center using the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{2+2}{2},\\frac{-1+1}{2})=(2,0)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=1$ and center $(a,b)=(2,0),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={1}^{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253308 :question/problem "![](images/05/0598fca48db1dbfb0cacadcf2ffb5cd987fc4e922fe85585849763fedac1f28e.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-3,0)$ and $(1,-2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-3,0)$ and $Y(1,-2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-3-1)^{2}+(0-(-2))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{2}=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-3+1}{2},\\frac{0-2}{2})=(-1,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{5}$ and center $(a,b)=(-1,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{5})}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253309 :question/problem "![](images/0a/0a0820b21672bbf16d2fdb35d708aefc4129fdc81c7594b55af3bd2ef5b8fc0f.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-2,0)$ and $(0,2)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-2,0)$ and $Y(0,2),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-2-0)^{2}+(0-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-2+0}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(-1,1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and center $(a,b)=(-1,1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-(-1))^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & ={(\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+(y-1)^{2} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253310 :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f1573d17cb9a603727b644da6598355875b0be4b8c43a382e562155cb7a095c.png)\n\nThe line segment $\\overset{―}{XY}$ is a diameter of a circle. Given that $X$ and $Y$ have coordinates $(-1,-3)$ and $(3,1)$, respectively, what is the equation of the circle?" :question/worked-solution "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the distance formula to compute the distance between the points $X(-1,-3)$ and $Y(3,1),$ we find that the length of the diameter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}XY & =\\sqrt{(-1-3)^{2}+(-3-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the radius of the circle is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the center of a circle is the midpoint of the diameter, to find the center we use the midpoint formula as follows:\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2},\\frac{{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}}{2})=(\\frac{-1+3}{2},\\frac{-3+1}{2})=(1,-1)\n$$\n\nSo our circle has radius $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ and center $(a,b)=(1,-1),$ and therefore it is represented by the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-a)^{2}+(y-b)^{2} & ={r}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y-(-1))^{2} & ={(2\\sqrt{2})}^{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+(y+1)^{2} & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253364 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{1}{4})?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$ \n\nSince $A=1,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|1|=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253366 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=4,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|4|=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253367 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=-7\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=-7,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|-7|=7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253368 :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=\\frac{3-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite the function as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{3-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{5}=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $A=-\\frac{9}{5}.$ Therefore, the amplitude is $|A|=|-\\frac{9}{5}|=\\frac{9}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253369 :question/problem "What is the period of the function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2\\pi }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253370 :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4x}{3}-{45}^{∘})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{4}{3}.$ Therefore, the period is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{360}^{∘}}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}={270}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253479 :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=x\\cdot {3}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)=x$ and $v(x)={3}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}\\cdot {3}^{x}+x\\cdot ({3}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+(x)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x}) \\\\  & ={3}^{x}+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\  & =(1+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3){3}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253570 :question/problem "$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }}+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta  \\\\  & =1+({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we simplified the right-hand side using ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}\\theta (\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta })+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253571 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}\\theta ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ) & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{5}\\theta ={\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253572 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253574 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta\n$$\n\nThen, we use ${\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}\\theta ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253575 :question/problem "$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253576 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta ({\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot 1+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot \\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{4}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta {\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{csc}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253577 :question/problem "$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot ({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta )} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{5}\\theta +{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}\\theta {\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253578 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 1+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}x}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}x}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}x+{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x{\\operatorname{sin}}^{3}x+\\operatorname{sin}x{\\operatorname{cos}}^{3}x+1}{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}=\\operatorname{cot}x+{\\operatorname{csc}}^{2}x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253594 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the first term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{sin}x}-\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-(\\operatorname{cos}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-\\operatorname{cos}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253596 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\theta \\operatorname{sec}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253599 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x+\\operatorname{sin}x+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)+\\operatorname{sin}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{2+\\operatorname{sin}x}+\\operatorname{sin}x=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253600 :question/problem "$1+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } & =\\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}{1\\cdot (1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta )}+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{sin}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta +{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } & =\\frac{1+1}{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1+\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}\\theta }{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }=\\frac{2}{1+{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}\\theta }.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253602 :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{1}-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x}-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x-{\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x-{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x-({\\operatorname{sin}}^{2}x+{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x-1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}x-\\frac{{\\operatorname{cos}}^{2}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}x}=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256387 :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,{225}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256389 :question/problem "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $6,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=6,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256407 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{12})^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=(12,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256409 :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256423 :question/problem "![](images/48/48517c5d3fa340bed1cc7b5493f0099a72afa79d9e0690b79695afe018c8a05c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=6.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $6,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $6.$ \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(6,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-3,3\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256424 :question/problem "![](images/5f/5ff0219f3f76441dbffc770ce0b8f2481cca8c46b8aa832ab7893d472f3026ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256425 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256426 :question/problem "![](images/47/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256656 :question/problem "Consider the line integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the segment of the line $3x=y$ traversed from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(3,9).$ Which of the following is the missing expression under the integral on the right?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve can be expressed in the form $x=x(y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3x=y\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{y}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{t}{3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{3},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}0\\le t\\le 9\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $0\\le t\\le 9$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{9}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{10}{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=9{x}^{2}y,$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =9{\\left(\\frac{t}{3}\\right)}^{2}(t) \\\\  & =9\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{9}\\right)(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}9{x}^{2}y\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{9}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{9}{\\color{blue}{t}^{3}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256657 :question/problem "![](images/6c/6cabe5598368b4f26ccca515d4f4011100e0bc410b72d919db9681b3145814fb.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$ where $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$ traversed between the points $(0,1)$ and $(2,{e}^{2}),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parameter $y=t.$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y).$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have \n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}t,\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le {e}^{2}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{t}^{2}}{{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{|t|} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)={e}^{x}{y}^{2},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}t}){t}^{2} \\\\  & =(t){t}^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x}{y}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}{t}^{2}\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256667 :question/problem "![](images/02/02582b9146af7bfa73ade5c4bd26ec7102650f324fede161f98482153730e868.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the parabola $x={y}^{2}$ from $(1,1)$ to $(6,\\sqrt{6}),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)={t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le \\sqrt{6}$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{12x}{y},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{12{t}^{2}}{t} \\\\  & =12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{12x}{y}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u=4{t}^{2}+1,$ $\\text{d}u=8t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{6}}12t\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =12{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{8}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{25}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{25}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =125-5\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =5(25-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256674 :question/problem "![](images/46/46f2f23c1cc2cb2caacd3703665af673a52a7e876665c7ba9420ae72138e1068.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the section of the curve $x=\\frac{2}{y}$ from $(2,1)$ to $(1,2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the given curve is of the form $x=x(y),$ Therefore, by setting $y=t,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=x(t)=\\frac{2}{t}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the following parametrization of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\frac{2}{t},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩,\\hspace{2em}1\\le t\\le 2\n$$\n\nNote that the domain $1\\le t\\le 2$ is defined by the $y$-coordinates of the end-points on the path.\n\nComputing ${\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)$ and $‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\ ‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{\\left(-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{{t}^{4}}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+4}{{t}^{4}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=x{y}^{6},$ then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\frac{2}{t}\\cdot {t}^{6} \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{6}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\hspace{0.167em}‖{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t)‖\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2{t}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve this using the substitution $u={t}^{4}+4,$ $\\text{d}u=4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{\\int }_{1}^{2}{t}^{3}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+4}\\,\\text{d}t & =2{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{5}^{20}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}\\,{|}_{5}^{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\sqrt{{20}^{3}}-\\sqrt{{5}^{3}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{40\\sqrt{5}-5\\sqrt{5}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{35\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256676 :question/problem "Find the gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient vector field of a function $f$ is given by $∇f.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the gradient vector field of $f(x,y,z)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{z}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
